WEBVTT

00:00:00.586 --> 00:00:24.498
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we're staying local — DFO just closed the door on any Fraser sockeye opening this season, forecasts cut from 7.7 million fish to under 2.8 million. Then we dig into Williams Lake, where a half-million-dollar asbestos fine at the Sandman Hotel raises a sharper question: who actually gets to catch a hazard before the fine shows up. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:01:02.561
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Saturday, August 29th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs.

00:01:03.356 --> 00:01:12.892
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're staying close to home — councils, permits, inspection reports, the unglamorous machinery that decides what happens on a given street in a given town.

00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:28.048
<v Riley>Quick weather sweep before we get into it. Horsefly Lake is sitting at 8 degrees with overcast skies this morning, high of 18 today, low of 8 tonight, and tomorrow's shaping up as light drizzle — barely any accumulation expected.

00:01:28.752 --> 00:01:42.016
<v Casey>Around the rest of the region — 100 Mile House is at 6 and overcast, Williams Lake at 8, Quesnel at 9, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Redstone is sitting at 7 with mostly clear skies. Cooler mornings now, that late-August turn.

00:01:42.384 --> 00:01:44.184
<v Riley>Let's get into what actually happened this week.

00:01:45.584 --> 00:02:30.408
<v Riley>It's a shorter list today, but two of these stories are dense enough that we're not going to rush past them. The DFO has confirmed there will be no recreational sockeye opening on the Fraser this season — the agency's language was "not happening at this time," which in practice means the door is closed for this run. Pre-season forecasts had predicted returns around 7.7 million fish. That's now been revised down to less than 2.8 million, with wildfires and drought named as contributing factors on top of the usual ocean-survival variables nobody fully controls. And it's not just sport fishers feeling this — sockeye runs feed commercial allocations and food, social, and ceremonial fisheries for Interior First Nations too.

00:02:31.762 --> 00:02:37.218
<v Casey>Not a maybe. A no. And a forecast cut of that size ripples through more than one user group at once.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:54.944
<v Riley>Second story close to home, and it's a Vancouver number with a Cariboo-shaped lesson in it. BC Hydro is spending 90 million dollars to upgrade the Mount Pleasant substation. A new transformer there will power fifty thousand additional homes once it's in service.

00:02:56.245 --> 00:03:16.389
<v Casey>Fifty thousand homes off one transformer — that's the scale provincial grid investment operates at in a dense urban core. When a Cariboo community goes looking for a capacity upgrade for a new subdivision or an industrial load, that's the size of project it's competing against for the same provincial capital budget and the same BC Hydro engineering queue.

00:03:17.763 --> 00:03:30.587
<v Riley>Worth remembering next time a rural upgrade request sits in a planning backlog for a couple of years. The bottleneck isn't necessarily indifference. Vancouver's substation math eats a lot of the available room.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:52.334
<v Casey>From there we're into the wider stack, and this next one's almost a parody of the moment we're in. An AI accounting startup called Rillet raised a hundred million dollars and became a unicorn in forty-eight hours. The CEO shared growth numbers at a routine board meeting, word got out, and it triggered a fundraising frenzy among investors including Iconiq and Sequoia.

00:03:53.637 --> 00:04:04.397
<v Riley>Forty-eight hours from an internal board update to a hundred-million-dollar valuation jump. Somewhere a rural credit union is still waiting on a callback about a farm equipment loan.

00:04:05.575 --> 00:04:22.407
<v Casey>The Verge has a rundown of everything changing on Instagram and Facebook for teenagers — new default privacy settings, tighter restrictions on who can message minors, limits on ad targeting aimed at younger users, all part of the child-safety settlement Meta reached with attorneys general across the United States.

00:04:23.779 --> 00:04:42.523
<v Riley>France went the opposite direction on the same underlying question this week. Its highest court struck down a national ban on children under fifteen using social media outright, ruling that it disproportionately limited free speech and didn't actually solve the privacy problems it claimed to address.

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:57.019
<v Casey>Two jurisdictions, same week, opposite conclusions on who decides what a teenager sees online — the platform under legal settlement, or the state trying to ban the platform outright. Neither one puts the decision in a parent's hands.

00:04:58.276 --> 00:05:15.268
<v Riley>Al Jazeera has the US Justice Department walking back part of its own story. Officials had suggested Chinese state hackers breached the US Senate and the Federal Reserve. The Department has now clarified both were targets of attempted intrusions, not confirmed successful breaches.

00:05:16.403 --> 00:05:31.507
<v Casey>The Vancouver Sun is calling it fortuitous timing — a new port facility from Ray-Mont Logistics is opening in Prince Rupert right as trade tensions with the US escalate again, adding Canadian export capacity just as Ottawa pushes to diversify away from American markets.

00:05:32.697 --> 00:05:51.297
<v Riley>Anything that gives interior producers another route to tidewater that isn't funneled entirely through American infrastructure matters here, even at a remove. Lumber, agricultural products, minerals — all of it eventually needs a port, and right now there are only a handful of realistic options on the coast.

00:05:52.449 --> 00:06:06.913
<v Casey>Eagle Feather News has Cowessess First Nation in Saskatchewan launching a 450,000 dollar circular food sovereignty project — turning waste streams into garden inputs, creating jobs, and building toward long-term food sustainability under their own governance.

00:06:08.108 --> 00:06:20.012
<v Riley>A Saskatchewan nation, not a Cariboo one, but the model is worth studying regardless of geography — a nation-run waste-to-food pipeline, fully under community control from design through operation.

00:06:21.187 --> 00:06:30.171
<v Casey>Al Jazeera also closed out a story worth a sigh of relief — crews finished pulling people out after landslides on a Peruvian highway had trapped them for nearly a week.

00:06:32.194 --> 00:06:56.906
<v Riley>On the lighter, more hands-on end of things — Hackaday has two pieces this week for anyone who likes fixing things for the sake of understanding them. One walks through reviving a dead SD card using shorted capacitors as a diagnostic trick. The other documents someone building a Hammond organ from scratch, purely to understand the electromechanical guts of how the instrument produces its sound.

00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:11.523
<v Casey>Engadget has a practical one for anyone living at the edge of a Wi-Fi signal out here — turning an old Android phone already sitting in a drawer into a Wi-Fi extender. No new hardware purchase required, just a settings change and some patience.

00:07:12.106 --> 00:07:14.634
<v Riley>Free fix for a problem half the Cariboo has.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:33.332
<v Casey>ScienceAlert has a strange one out of the research world — scientists describing a newly discovered way life can build DNA-like molecular structures without conventional DNA at all. Early findings, still being worked through, but a genuine curiosity if you like your science on the weirder end of the spectrum.

00:07:34.659 --> 00:07:59.283
<v Riley>And Hyperallergic has a retrospective worth closing on — a look back at how AIDS activists in New York in the 1980s used stark, DIY graphic design as a form of direct political pressure. Wheat-pasted posters, stickers, flyers designed to be unmissable. Keith Haring's work is part of that story — design used as an organizing tool decades before anyone called it activism through visual culture.

00:08:00.459 --> 00:08:04.435
<v Casey>Good place to land the roundup. Let's move into today's Community Spotlight.

00:08:05.835 --> 00:08:33.155
<v Casey>Today we're highlighting the Quesnel Women's Resource Centre. They're a feminist collective offering stopping-the-violence counselling, a free Thursday wellness clinic, drop-in services, and advocacy work for women and families across the region. It's the kind of organization that keeps a community held together without ever needing a press release to justify itself — steady hours, people who show up, a door that's actually open when someone needs it. You can find them online at qwrc.ca.

00:08:33.727 --> 00:08:42.047
<v Riley>That free Thursday clinic alone is worth knowing about if you're anywhere near Quesnel and need a door that doesn't require an appointment three weeks out.

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:50.496
<v Casey>Which, oddly enough, is a good bridge into today's Deep Dive — because it's also about who gets to open a door, and who has to ask permission first.

00:08:51.896 --> 00:09:16.384
<v Riley>Two stories out of Williams Lake this week look unrelated on the surface. WorkSafeBC fined the Sandman Hotel and Suites, operated by Northland Properties Corporation, over 624,000 dollars for asbestos-related workplace safety violations. And separately, a cannabis retailer called Marlee's Den has applied to relocate into the old Warehouse One space at Prosperity Ridge Shopping Centre.

00:09:16.928 --> 00:09:22.688
<v Casey>One's a safety enforcement action. The other's a real estate application. What's actually connecting them?

00:09:22.982 --> 00:09:38.854
<v Riley>Both are about who has standing to say yes or no when a building changes. In one case, that authority sits entirely with a provincial agency nobody in Williams Lake ever voted for. In the other, it sits with city council, where a resident can actually show up and speak.

00:09:39.132 --> 00:09:41.420
<v Casey>Say the real question you're circling, then.

00:09:42.052 --> 00:09:55.988
<v Riley>When a fine over half a million dollars lands after the fact, who was actually positioned to catch the problem before it became one? The province? The corporate owner? Or nobody local, because nobody local ever had the authority to act?

00:09:56.287 --> 00:10:10.023
<v Casey>Northland Properties runs Sandman hotels across the country. WorkSafeBC's report is specific to the Williams Lake property — asbestos risk in an occupied, operating hotel. That's a national chain cited for a hazard at one location among dozens.

00:10:10.645 --> 00:10:26.901
<v Riley>Asbestos abatement legally requires certified contractors, and that licensing regime exists for a real reason. Untrained removal disturbs the material and spreads fibers into the air instead of containing them. The certification requirement is functioning as intended.

00:10:27.549 --> 00:10:40.301
<v Casey>Except the fine arrived after WorkSafeBC found the violation during an inspection, not before anyone was exposed. The certification regime didn't prevent the hazard. It priced it, after the fact, once it was already discovered.

00:10:40.976 --> 00:10:47.808
<v Riley>A fine at that scale is meant to deter the next building's owner, not undo the exposure that already happened at this one.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:58.502
<v Casey>Deter who, though? Northland operates hotel properties in multiple provinces. A six-figure fine against one property is a rounding error against that company's total portfolio value.

00:10:59.126 --> 00:11:16.422
<v Riley>The real structural gap is this: WorkSafeBC has the jurisdiction and the technical expertise to identify and penalize this kind of violation. What it doesn't have is a continuous local presence in Williams Lake that would've flagged the material years before an inspection was ever triggered.

00:11:16.938 --> 00:11:31.722
<v Casey>And the City of Williams Lake has zero formal role in any of it. No building inspector on staff monitoring hazardous materials in older commercial buildings. No municipal bylaw requiring disclosure of known hazards before a change of use or an ownership transfer.

00:11:32.283 --> 00:11:53.123
<v Riley>Local governments generally don't have the technical capacity to run their own hazmat inspection program. Asbestos identification requires lab analysis, trained samplers, chain-of-custody documentation. Every small town in the province having its own certified hazmat inspector on permanent payroll isn't realistic for a city this size.

00:11:53.739 --> 00:12:03.851
<v Casey>Nobody's proposing that. The current system has exactly one lever — punishment after discovery — and no lever at all for prevention that a Cariboo-sized town could pull on its own.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:20.628
<v Riley>Compare that to what's happening with the cannabis relocation. Marlee's Den needs a development permit, a zoning compliance check, likely a public notice period before council votes. Council has direct, enforceable authority over every part of that process.

00:12:21.221 --> 00:12:32.189
<v Casey>So council has real teeth over a retail store moving three hundred meters down the road, and none over asbestos sitting in an occupied hotel built decades before anyone currently on council was elected.

00:12:32.874 --> 00:12:46.570
<v Riley>The asbestos was almost certainly installed under a building code that predates most of today's hazardous materials disclosure requirements. The hazard itself is older than the governance tools eventually built to catch it.

00:12:47.237 --> 00:12:56.085
<v Casey>Then the fine isn't punishing negligence in any meaningful sense. It's punishing a building for outliving the regulatory framework it was originally constructed under.

00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:12.300
<v Riley>It's both. Regardless of when the building went up, Northland still carries an ongoing legal obligation to manage known risk in a hotel occupied by paying guests and staff right now. Age of construction doesn't excuse a current operator from a current safety duty.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:20.008
<v Casey>Sure, but who was checking in the years between the original construction and this year's inspection? That's the empty chair in this whole story.

00:13:20.623 --> 00:13:33.079
<v Riley>Nobody, structurally speaking. WorkSafeBC responds to complaints and runs scheduled audits on some rotation. It isn't running continuous, building-by-building monitoring of every hotel property across the Interior.

00:13:33.598 --> 00:13:44.886
<v Casey>Which means the real enforcement trigger for a hazard in a Williams Lake hotel is either a worker filing a formal complaint, or the pure timing luck of a scheduled audit landing on the right building in the right year.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:58.332
<v Riley>That's not unique to asbestos, either. Most provincial safety regimes in Canada work this way — reactive rather than preventative, because continuous inspection at true scale has never been funded anywhere in this country.

00:13:58.891 --> 00:14:09.507
<v Casey>Then the honest answer to who's allowed to fix a hazard like this one is: whoever WorkSafeBC happens to certify, whenever WorkSafeBC happens to look. Nobody local gets a vote in the timing.

00:14:10.084 --> 00:14:24.812
<v Riley>And there's no formal local channel to speed that timing up. A hotel worker who notices deteriorating material in a hallway has no city office to escalate to that would reach WorkSafeBC any faster than dialing the provincial hotline themselves.

00:14:25.371 --> 00:14:42.875
<v Casey>The gap worth naming clearly isn't the certification requirement — that part is doing exactly the job it was built for, keeping untrained people away from a dangerous removal process. The gap is the empty space between "the building exists with a hazard in it" and "the province eventually gets around to inspecting it."

00:14:43.509 --> 00:15:01.045
<v Riley>Now compare that to the zoning process running in parallel for Marlee's Den. Any resident in Williams Lake can show up to a council meeting and raise a concern about that relocation before council votes on it. There's a built-in public comment window as a formal step in that process.

00:15:01.623 --> 00:15:12.127
<v Casey>So a cannabis store relocating into a strip mall carries more built-in public voice, by design, than a hazardous material sitting in an occupied hotel for however many years it sat there undetected.

00:15:12.766 --> 00:15:29.254
<v Riley>Because zoning was designed from the outset with local input as a core feature. Provincial safety regulation was designed around technical expertise and centralized enforcement, not public participation. They're different tools, built for different kinds of problems.

00:15:29.779 --> 00:15:38.387
<v Casey>Different tools, sure, but the practical outcome for a resident is the same shape of gap either way — legitimate concern, and nowhere close to home to take it.

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:49.262
<v Riley>So what would actually close that gap? You can't put untrained municipal staff on hazmat inspection duty — that just creates a different kind of liability problem for the city.

00:15:49.836 --> 00:16:09.628
<v Casey>You don't need trained inspectors locally to fix this. You need a formal referral path — a bylaw officer with explicit authority to log a safety complaint on a commercial building and forward it to WorkSafeBC for review, instead of leaving residents to track down the provincial number and file the complaint cold, with no local record it was ever raised.

00:16:10.288 --> 00:16:28.480
<v Riley>That's a concrete, testable policy ask, at least — Williams Lake council could pass a bylaw requiring staff to log any safety complaint about a commercial building and forward it to the relevant provincial body within a set number of business days, with the referral logged in a public record.

00:16:29.136 --> 00:16:39.200
<v Casey>Cheap to implement, too. No new certifications, no new inspectors, no new budget line. Just a documented handoff process that currently doesn't exist anywhere in the city's structure.

00:16:39.828 --> 00:16:53.652
<v Riley>It also creates an accountability trail that doesn't exist right now. If a complaint sat unaddressed for six months before anyone referred it upward, that delay becomes visible and answerable in a way "nobody knew about it" currently isn't.

00:16:54.279 --> 00:17:03.599
<v Casey>I'll take that over another grant application as an outcome. It's a governance fix, not a funding fix, and that's a different kind of ask to bring to a council meeting.

00:17:04.283 --> 00:17:17.187
<v Riley>The Prosperity Ridge relocation is a lower-stakes version of the same lesson. The process that works is the one where a resident knows who to call, and knows there's a deadline attached to what happens next.

00:17:17.847 --> 00:17:37.279
<v Casey>And the process that fails is the one where the only feedback loop a resident ever gets is a fine that surfaces years later in a newspaper article, after the exposure already happened. Though I'll admit, a strip mall cannabis relocation getting more due process than a hazardous hotel is the kind of detail that shouldn't be as funny as it is.

00:17:37.821 --> 00:17:56.957
<v Riley>Raise that directly at the next council meeting — ask whether a formal complaint-to-WorkSafeBC referral policy currently exists for commercial buildings in the city, and if it doesn't, ask why not. If you end up putting that question to them yourself, send us what they say back — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:17:57.469 --> 00:18:03.133
<v Casey>We'd want to hear the answer either way. And if the answer's "no policy exists," that's the story right there.

00:18:03.428 --> 00:18:05.092
<v Riley>Hope you're having a great weekend.

00:18:05.397 --> 00:18:08.053
<v Casey>Stay dry out there — that drizzle's rolling in tomorrow.